1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,136
Who decides the bank's fee on a payment?

2
00:00:02,186 --> 00:00:06,648
In Lesson 2, the acquirer kept its fee and paid the shop the rest.

3
00:00:06,698 --> 00:00:11,832
In Lesson 21, each website got its own deal: its cards and its limits.

4
00:00:11,882 --> 00:00:16,248
This lesson decides that fee, for every website and every card.

5
00:00:16,298 --> 00:00:21,432
Picture the fee written straight into the payment code, one rate for everyone.

6
00:00:21,482 --> 00:00:24,072
Then one merchant agrees a lower rate.

7
00:00:24,122 --> 00:00:26,520
One website gets a special offer.

8
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:28,512
One card costs the bank more.

9
00:00:28,562 --> 00:00:31,608
Soon the code is a tangle nobody dares touch.

10
00:00:31,658 --> 00:00:37,128
Worse, someone changes a rate in place, and last month's payments can no longer be explained.

11
00:00:37,178 --> 00:00:43,176
In a supermarket, the tag on the item beats the sign on the aisle, which beats the offer for the whole store.

12
00:00:43,226 --> 00:00:45,264
The most specific price wins.

13
00:00:45,314 --> 00:00:47,472
The bank's fee works the same way.

14
00:00:47,522 --> 00:00:49,344
Here is how it works.

15
00:00:49,394 --> 00:00:52,272
A fee rule is one price the bank sets.

16
00:00:52,322 --> 00:00:56,976
It can apply to one website, one merchant, one card, or the whole bank.

17
00:00:57,026 --> 00:01:00,504
When a payment arrives, the most specific rule wins.

18
00:01:00,554 --> 00:01:03,624
A website's own rule beats its merchant's rule.

19
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:06,336
A merchant's rule beats a card's rule.

20
00:01:06,386 --> 00:01:10,752
And the bank's default is used only when nothing more specific applies.

21
00:01:10,802 --> 00:01:14,664
The fee itself is a percent of the amount, plus a fixed part.

22
00:01:14,714 --> 00:01:17,112
It is kept between a minimum and a maximum.

23
00:01:17,162 --> 00:01:21,624
Money is always counted in whole cents, never in fractions of a cent.

24
00:01:21,674 --> 00:01:26,616
So the percent is rounded once, to whole cents, and the sum is exact.

25
00:01:26,666 --> 00:01:28,992
Rules are never edited in place.

26
00:01:29,042 --> 00:01:32,904
A change is proposed by one admin, and a second admin approves it.

27
00:01:32,954 --> 00:01:39,552
Two rules for the same thing can never apply at the same moment: the database itself refuses an overlap.

28
00:01:39,602 --> 00:01:44,544
And every payment keeps the rule it used, its fee, and what the merchant receives.

29
00:01:44,594 --> 00:01:49,104
So last month's payments can always be explained, whatever the rules say today.

30
00:01:49,154 --> 00:01:50,976
Now watch the product.

31
00:01:51,026 --> 00:01:54,696
The viewer signs in as a bank admin and opens Fees.

32
00:01:54,746 --> 00:02:01,632
The page says it plainly: the most specific rule wins, and a new rule applies after a second admin approves it.

33
00:02:01,682 --> 00:02:06,648
Filters show the rules in force now, the ones waiting for approval, or all of them.

34
00:02:06,698 --> 00:02:08,520
The viewer clicks New rule.

35
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:13,584
Under "Applies to" there are three choices: merchant, website and card.

36
00:02:13,634 --> 00:02:17,376
A hint says to leave everything empty for the bank default.

37
00:02:17,426 --> 00:02:23,136
If the viewer picks a merchant but no card, the form says a merchant or website rule needs a card.

38
00:02:23,186 --> 00:02:29,424
The viewer leaves the scope empty, sets a percent, a fixed part and a minimum, and clicks Send for approval.

39
00:02:29,474 --> 00:02:32,400
The page says another admin has to approve it.

40
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:37,056
In Approvals, under Fee changes, the maker's own buttons are grey.

41
00:02:37,106 --> 00:02:41,496
A second admin signs in, opens the same tab, and clicks Approve.

42
00:02:41,546 --> 00:02:47,424
Back on Fees, the rule now shows as in force, with who proposed it and who approved it.

43
00:02:47,474 --> 00:02:49,296
Now the Fee calculator.

44
00:02:49,346 --> 00:02:52,344
Its hint says it shows what a payment would pay right now.

45
00:02:52,394 --> 00:02:56,760
The viewer chooses a merchant and a website, because a quote needs both.

46
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:58,752
The amount is already filled in.

47
00:02:58,802 --> 00:03:00,624
They click Calculate.

48
00:03:00,674 --> 00:03:05,688
The answer shows the fee, the rule used, and how much the merchant receives.

49
00:03:05,738 --> 00:03:07,560
What does this protect?

50
00:03:07,610 --> 00:03:10,992
Without rules, every new deal is a change to the code.

51
00:03:11,042 --> 00:03:15,432
Without four eyes, one admin could quietly give a friend a lower rate.

52
00:03:15,482 --> 00:03:20,376
Without whole cents, small fractions would leak away across millions of payments.

53
00:03:20,426 --> 00:03:26,376
And without the rule kept on each payment, nobody could answer a merchant who asks why they paid what they paid.

54
00:03:26,426 --> 00:03:30,072
Fee rules protect merchants, the bank and its auditors.

55
00:03:30,122 --> 00:03:35,424
So, like price tags: the most specific wins, and nobody repaints the old ones.

56
00:03:35,474 --> 00:03:39,240
Next: the virtual card machine each payment goes through.
